    <!-- Why strict -->
    <string name="why_strict_title">Why so strict?</string>
    <string name="why_strict_intro">None of this is here to annoy you — every constraint has a specific reason.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point1_title">No snooze button</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point1_body">Falling back asleep for a few minutes fragments your sleep and leaves you groggier at the next wake-up — you often feel more tired after snoozing than before.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point2_title">The same time every day</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point2_body">An irregular wake-up time throws off your internal clock far more than slightly less sleep now and then. Regularity matters more than exact duration.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point3_title">Light that rises before the alarm</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point3_body">Gradually rising light before the alarm prepares your body to wake up, using your eyes\' sensitivity to hold back melatonin before the alarm even sounds.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point4_title">A bit earlier if you\'re already in light sleep</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point4_body">Waking up during a light sleep phase instead of deep sleep makes waking up far less brutal — hence the \"from X if in light sleep\" window.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point5_title">A QR code to scan, not a button</string>
    <string name="why_strict_point5_body">The only real defense against the urge to fall back asleep is a gesture that requires you to actually get up and move — not something within reach in bed.</string>
    <string name="why_strict_closing">Goal: an alarm that actually works, even on the days you don\'t feel like it.</string>

